From: Emre Yavuzalp <emreyavuzalp@gmail.com>
Subject: econet-eth: raise BQL min limit, TX was serialized to one packet

TX-done completions arrive with a multi-millisecond latency on this
hardware (~3.2 ms; they appear to ride on a periodic tick rather than
firing promptly per packet). BQL observes that latency and clamps every
TX queue's byte limit down to a single packet (~1686 bytes), which
serializes transmission to one packet per completion round-trip:
~314 packets/s regardless of packet size, i.e. ~3.6 Mbit/s with
1400-byte UDP while RX does ~131 Mbit/s on the same link.

The hardware itself is fast: with the BQL clamp lifted the same test
does 91.8 Mbit/s with 0% loss and 0.089 ms jitter (measured live via
/sys/class/net/eth0/queues/tx-*/byte_queue_limits/limit_min on a
TP-Link Archer C5v, EN751221).

Raise the per-queue BQL floor above the TX ring capacity
(128 descriptors * ~1514 bytes ~= 194 KB) so the ring, not BQL, is the
limiter. The netdev registers 256 soft queues, so this must be applied
to every queue.

--- a/econet_port.c
+++ b/econet_port.c
@@ -145,6 +145,20 @@
 
 	netif_tx_start_all_queues(dev);
 
+	/* TX-done completions are reported with ~3 ms latency. BQL reacts
+	 * by clamping each queue's limit to a single packet, serializing
+	 * TX to one packet per completion round-trip (~314 pps). Keep the
+	 * per-queue floor above the TX ring capacity so the ring stays
+	 * full; this takes TX from ~3.6 Mbit/s to ~92 Mbit/s. */
+#ifdef CONFIG_BQL
+	{
+		unsigned int i;
+
+		for (i = 0; i < dev->num_tx_queues; i++)
+			netdev_get_tx_queue(dev, i)->dql.min_limit = 200000;
+	}
+#endif
+
